Title:
MSRKit — Kernel function caller & unsigned driver mapper via WRMSR/LSTAR ROP (BYOVD AmdTools64.sys)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
MSRKit is a Windows post-exploitation tool/library that leverages a vulnerable driver (AmdTools64.sys) to gain an MSR read/write primitive and pivot into kernel-mode execution. It reads IA32_LSTAR, finds ROP gadgets in ntoskrnl.exe, overwrites LSTAR to a gadget, and triggers a syscall to execute a kernel ROP chain. The chain temporarily disables SMEP/SMAP via CR4 manipulation, calls arbitrary kernel exports or addresses, then restores CR4 and LSTAR and returns to user mode via sysretq. It also implements an unsigned driver mapper: allocates nonpaged pool, copies a prepared PE (relocs/imports/cookie), and calls the mapped entry point. Useful for red teams/pentesters and kernel security researchers exploring BYOVD-based kernel execution and driver mapping; notable for working across Windows 10/11 builds (with HVCI disabled) and handling KPTI constraints.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD using AmdTools64.sys MSR IOCTLs (WRMSR/RDMSR)
- IA32_LSTAR hijacking and syscall entrypoint redirection
- Kernel ROP chain construction and gadget scanning in ntoskrnl.exe
- SMEP/SMAP bypass by toggling CR4 bits; FMASK/AC handling
- Unsigned driver mapping in kernel pool (relocations, imports, security cookie patching)
- User↔kernel transition control (syscall/sysretq) and stability constraints (single-core pinning)

Use Cases:
- Call ntoskrnl exports (e.g., ExAllocatePool2) from user mode via kernel ROP
- Invoke arbitrary kernel virtual addresses for research or exploitation
- Map and execute unsigned kernel drivers without standard loading mechanisms
- Build custom post-exploitation primitives (read/write, token manipulation) on top of kernel calls
- Evaluate/validate detections for LSTAR tampering, MSR abuse, and BYOVD activity

Keywords:
MSR, WRMSR, RDMSR, IA32_LSTAR, syscall, sysretq, ntoskrnl.exe, ROP chain, kernel gadgets, CR4, SMEP, SMAP, FMASK, AC flag, BYOVD, AmdTools64.sys, NtLoadDriver, IOCTL 0xFFF02804, ExAllocatePoolWithTag, ExAllocatePool2, nonpaged pool, driver mapping, KPTI, HVCI disabled, Windows 10, Windows 11